# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 3: Weather Description
#
# The WHEN task for the winter street photo of sub-lessons 1–2: the weather, and
# a guess at the season, the day and the time of day. The examiner asks it on
# almost every picture, and it is the one place in this theme where the candidate
# is allowed to INFER rather than report — so the point of this file is the
# hedging vocabulary, not the weather words.
#
# This is also where the two picture themes pay each other off. Schueberfouer is
# a sunny summer afternoon; this photo is a grey winter day. The hedge frames are
# the same, the answers are opposite — so a learner who has played both has to
# read the evidence rather than recall which season "the picture theme" is about.
#
# The photo detail this describes: a pale grey overcast sky, no sun and no
# shadows, no rain and dry cobbles, bare trees at the far end, everyone in thick
# coats, hats and scarves. No Christmas lights over the street, which is the
# evidence for the one negative inference below.
#
# The source description's reasoning is preserved as the file's spine, because it
# is exactly what the exam rewards — a claim plus the visible evidence for it:
#   "I think it is winter, BECAUSE the people are wearing warm clothes"
#   "but I think this is not Christmas — there are no Christmas lights"
#   "the weather is cold, but it is not raining"
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 and 02 — no @question prompts, no first-person
# opinion. A hedged inference *about the photo* ("it is probably winter") is not
# an opinion: it is an observation with a confidence marker, and a stranger can
# still check it against the picture. Nothing about the learner liking winter or
# this street belongs here, and nothing of that shape was authored.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as the rest of the theme: present tense, simple main
# clauses, and no attributive adjectives on masculine or neuter nouns — which is
# why the sky is `Den Himmel ass gro.` and not `en groen Himmel`.
#
# Words sub-lessons 1–2 already teach (d'Bild, d'Strooss, d'Leit, d'Kleeder,
# waarm, gesinn, mengen, …) are deliberately NOT repeated — the pass-gate makes
# them prerequisites and stat keys are shared across the app. `waarm` in
# particular is taught in 02 with the clothes it belongs to and is only *used*
# here, in the `well`-justification.
#
# The @fill blocks are this theme's highest-value frames, because a hedge is a
# pattern that transfers to every picture and every topic. Three of the six
# hedges the samples use are drilled here — `Ech sinn net sécher, mee villäicht`,
# `Et kéint … sinn`, and `Et gesäit no … aus` — deliberately NOT the two the
# Schueberfouer theme already owns (`Ech mengen, …` and `Et ass warscheinlech …`).
# Each has a comma before its blank, which is also why the Eifeler Regel cannot
# bite in them.

@lesson P2.03 "Groussgaass: Weather Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# the photo during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it
# describes the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/groussgaass/img/groussgaass.webp"
@image-alt "The same winter street photo, seen for the weather: a pale grey overcast sky with no sun and no shadows, dry cobblestones, bare trees at the far end of the street, and everyone in thick coats, hats and scarves."

# --- Weather ---
@word d'Wieder = the weather
@word den Himmel = the sky
@word d'Sonn = the sun
@word de Reen = the rain
@word d'Wolleken = the clouds
@word kal = cold
@word gro = grey
@word bedeckt = overcast
@word dréchen = dry
@word reenen = to rain

# --- Season, day and time of day ---
@word de Wanter = the winter
@word de Summer = the summer
@word d'Joreszäit = the season
@word de Chrëschtdag = Christmas Day
@word d'Luuchten = the lights
@word de Dag = the day
@word d'Woch = the week
@word d'Blieder = the leaves
@word gutt = good

# --- Hedging a guess ---
@word villäicht = maybe
@word warscheinlech = probably
@word sécher = certain
@word well = because

@sentence
@lu D'Wieder ass kal, mee et reent net.
@en The weather is cold, but it is not raining.
@distractor-lu waarm
@distractor-lu Sonn
@distractor-en warm
@distractor-en sun

@sentence
@lu Den Himmel ass gro a bedeckt.
@en The sky is grey and overcast.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu Sonn
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en sun

@sentence
@lu Um Bild ass keng Sonn.
@en In the picture there is no sun.
@distractor-lu Wolleken
@distractor-lu vill
@distractor-en clouds
@distractor-en many

@sentence
@lu D'Strooss ass dréchen.
@en The street is dry.
@distractor-lu naass
@distractor-lu kal
@distractor-en wet
@distractor-en cold

@sentence
@lu Ech mengen, et ass Wanter, well d'Leit waarm Kleeder unhunn.
@en I think it is winter, because the people have warm clothes on.
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu dréchen
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en dry

@sentence
@lu Op de Beem gesinn ech keng Blieder.
@en On the trees I see no leaves.
@distractor-lu vill
@distractor-lu Wolleken
@distractor-en many
@distractor-en clouds

@sentence
@lu Ech mengen, dëst ass net de Chrëschtdag.
@en I think this is not Christmas Day.
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-lu Woch
@distractor-en winter
@distractor-en week

@sentence
@lu Et gëtt keng Luuchten an der Strooss.
@en There are no lights in the street.
@distractor-lu vill
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-en many
@distractor-en trees

@sentence
@lu D'Joreszäit ass warscheinlech de Wanter.
@en The season is probably winter.
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en weather

@sentence
@lu Fir de Wanter ass d'Wieder gutt.
@en For winter the weather is good.
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu kal
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en cold

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# Hedge 1 of 3: the strongest disclaimer in the set, for a guess the photo only
# weakly supports. The comma both marks the clause boundary and exempts the blank
# from the Eifeler Regel — `mee villäicht ass et [Wanter]` would otherwise be
# unfixable after `-n`-final words. Season nouns are the blank; both distractors
# are seasons the bare trees and thick coats contradict.
@fill
@lu Ech sinn net sécher, mee villäicht ass et [Wanter].
@en I am not sure, but maybe it is [winter].
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu Hierscht
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en autumn

# Hedge 2 of 3: `Et kéint … sinn` — the subjunctive hedge, with the verb at the
# end, which is exactly the order a learner should read rather than assemble. Two
# blanks in DIFFERENT CLAUSES (the second is inside the `well`-clause), so they
# cannot swap — R6 satisfied by position, not by luck.
#
# The library's version of this frame blanks `[Dag]` after `en normalen`, and R5
# rejects it: `normalen` is `-n`-final mid-clause, so the correct frame text would
# depend on which tile is placed. Moving the blank one slot right to `an der
# [Woch]` fixes it and improves the item — the fixed `der` now admits only a
# feminine tile, so masculine `Weekend` is out on grammar alone (R4).
@fill
@lu Et kéint en normalen Dag an der [Woch] sinn, well vill [Leit] ënnerwee sinn.
@en It could be a normal day in the [week], because a lot of [people] are on the way.
@distractor-lu Weekend
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-lu Wolleken
@distractor-en weekend
@distractor-en trees
@distractor-en clouds

# Hedge 3 of 3: the split `gesäit no … aus`, the one hedge that is a verb bracket
# rather than an adverb. The bracket is the pattern, so it stays in the frame.
# Same R5 fix as hedge 2: the blank sits after `am`, not after the `-n`-final
# `kalen`, so the frame has one correct spelling regardless of the tile.
#
# Distractor choice is load-bearing here and is where the first authored fills in
# this app went wrong: a hedge slot must NEVER get another hedge as a distractor,
# because the six hedges are mutually substitutable and a second one would be a
# second CORRECT answer rather than a wrong one. These are season and weather
# nouns instead — `Reen` cannot follow `am` in this frame at all, and `Summer`
# contradicts the `kalen` already fixed in the frame.
@fill
@lu Et gesäit no engem kalen Dag am [Wanter] aus.
@en It looks like a cold day in [winter].
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu Fréijoer
@distractor-lu Reen
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en spring
@distractor-en rain

# The negative-evidence frame — a claim plus the absence that supports it, which
# is the shape of the source description's Christmas reasoning and transfers to
# any "is it a holiday / is it raining / is it busy" guess. Two blanks in
# different clauses and different word classes (season noun, then plural noun
# after the fixed `keng`). `keng` forces a plural, so singular tiles fail on
# agreement as well as on sense.
@fill
@lu Et ass warscheinlech net [Chrëschtdag], well et keng [Luuchten] an der Strooss ginn.
@en It is probably not [Christmas], because there are no [lights] in the street.
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-lu Blieder
@distractor-en winter
@distractor-en leaves
